Dependency Injection
----------------------
Dependency Injection (DI) in Spring is a design pattern where an external container manages and provides
the required dependencies of a class, instead of the class creating them internally.

It is a concrete implementation of Inversion of Control (IoC), meaning the control of object creation and
lifecycle management is shifted from the developer's manual code to the Spring Framework.

The Core Problem and the DI Solution
---------------------------------------
In standard Java, if a Car class needs an Engine, we typically use the new keyword inside the Car class:

public class Car{
    private Engine engine = new Engine(); //Tightly coupled
}

This causes tight coupling; if the Engine constructor changes, the Car class breaks.

With Spring DI, the Car class simply declares that it needs an Engine, and the Spring IoC Container automatically
supplies (injects) that Engine instance at runtime:

@Component
public class Car{

    private final Engine engine;

    //Spring automatically injects the Engine bean here
    public Car(Engine engine){
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}

Note: IoC is the broader principle where control of object creation and management is transferred to the Spring Container.
      Dependency Injection is the mechanism through which Spring provides the required dependencies to objects.

3 Main Types of Dependency Injection
-------------------------------------

Spring supports three primary variants to inject dependencies into a class:

1. Constructor Injection (Recommended)

    Dependencies are provided through the class constructor.
    This is the industry-standard choice because it ensures required dependencies are never null and allows fields
    to be marked as final (promoting immutability).

Example 1:

@Service
public class EmployeeService{

    private final EmployeeRepository empRepo;

    //@Autowired annotation is optional is the class has only one constructor
    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository eRepo){
        this.empRepo = eRepo;
    }

}

Example 2:

@Component
public class OrderService{

    private final PaymentService;

    //@Autowired annotation is optional is the class has only one constructor
    public OrderService(PaymentService ps){
        this.PaymentService = ps;
    }
}

2. Setter Injection

    Dependencies are supplied through public setter methods.
    This approach is ideal for optional or modifiable dependencies that can be changed or assigned later.

Example:

@Component
public class OrderService{

    private PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor;

    @Autowired
    public void setPaymentProcessor(PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor){
        this.paymentProcessor = paymentProcessor;
    }
}

3. Field Injection

    Dependencies are injected directly into the fields using reflection by placing the @Autowired annotation right on the variable.
    While convenient and brief, it is generally discouraged because it hides dependencies and makes unit testing
    outside of a Spring container difficult.

Example:

@Component
public class EmployeeService{

    @Autowired
    private EmployeeRepository employeeRepository;
}

The difference between Dependency Injection and IoC container
--------------------------------------------------------------

IoC is the broader principle where control of object creation and management is transferred to the Spring Container.
Dependency Injection is the mechanism through which Spring provides the required dependencies to objects.

Note: Constructor injection is generally preferred because dependencies are explicit,
      required dependencies can be made final, and the class is easier to test.

      @Autowired tells Spring where a dependency needs to be injected.
      OR It tells Spring to inject a suitable dependency into a constructor, field, or setter.

@Component,@Controller, @RestController, @Service, @Repository
----------------------------------------------------------------

These are all ways of telling Spring:
"This class should be managed as a Spring bean."

@Component: A generic Spring-managed component
@Controller: Used for Spring MVC controllers
@RestController: Commonly used for REST APIs
@Service: Typically used for business logic
@Repository: Used for data-access classes

What happens when a spring-boot application starts?
--------------------------------------------------
When the Spring Boot application starts, SpringApplication.run() creates and initializes the
Spring ApplicationContext.

Through component scanning, Spring discovers classes annotated with components such as @Service, @Repository, and @Controller
and registers them as beans.

Spring then creates and configures those beans and resolves their dependencies through Dependency Injection.

Auto-configuration configures the required Spring infrastructure based on the application's dependencies.
Once the context is initialized, the application is ready to handle requests

@SpringBootApplication
        |
Spring Boot starts
        |
ApplicationContext
        |
Component Scanning
        |
Find @Service / @Repository / @Controller
        |
Register Beans
        |
Create Beans
        |
Resolve Dependencies
        |
Dependency Injection
        |
Application Ready

The difference between @Controller and @RestController
-------------------------------------------------------

@Controller and @RestController are used to handle HTTP requests in a Spring application.
The main difference is what we want to return to the client.
@Controller is mainly used when we want to return a web page or view, like an HTML page.
For example, if a user requests /employees, the controller can return an employees.html page.
On the other hand, @RestController is mainly used for building REST APIs, where we want to return data, usually in JSON format,
instead of a web page. For example, if we call /employees, it can return a list of employees as JSON.
@RestController is basically a combination of @Controller and @ResponseBody. @ResponseBody tells Spring to put the returned Java object
directly into the HTTP response, and Spring converts it into JSON. So, in a Spring Boot microservices application, we generally use
@RestController because our services communicate through REST APIs and exchange JSON data.

The easiest way to remember it is: @Controller -> return a page, @RestController -> return data/JSON.

Why do we use @RestController?: We use @RestController to create REST APIs that handle HTTP requests and return data, usually JSON,
                                directly in the HTTP response. It is convenient because it combines @Controller and @ResponseBody.
